@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1147 = private unnamed_addr constant [670 x i8] c"## Removed\0AThis rule has been removed because type checkers can infer this type without annotation.\0A\0A## What it does\0AChecks that instance method `self` arguments have type annotations.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AType annotations are a good way to document the types of function arguments. They also\0Ahelp catch bugs, when used alongside a type checker, by ensuring that the types of\0Aany provided arguments match expectation.\0A\0ANote that many type checkers will infer the type of `self` automatically, so this\0Aannotation is not strictly necessary.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Aclass Foo:\0A    def bar(self): ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Aclass Foo:\0A    def bar(self: \22Foo\22): ...\0A```\0A", align 1
@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1148 = private unnamed_addr constant [16 x i8] c"missing-type-cls", align 1
@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1149 = private unnamed_addr constant [703 x i8] c"## Removed\0AThis rule has been removed because type checkers can infer this type without annotation.\0A\0A## What it does\0AChecks that class method `cls` arguments have type annotations.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AType annotations are a good way to document the types of function arguments. They also\0Ahelp catch bugs, when used alongside a type checker, by ensuring that the types of\0Aany provided arguments match expectation.\0A\0ANote that many type checkers will infer the type of `cls` automatically, so this\0Aannotation is not strictly necessary.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Aclass Foo:\0A    @classmethod\0A    def bar(cls): ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Aclass Foo:\0A    @classmethod\0A    def bar(cls: Type[\22Foo\22]): ...\0A```\0A", align 1
@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1150 = private unnamed_addr constant [48 x i8] c"missing-return-type-undocumented-public-function", align 1
@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1151 = private unnamed_addr constant [804 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks that public functions and methods have return type annotations.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AType annotations are a good way to document the return types of functions. They also\0Ahelp catch bugs, when used alongside a type checker, by ensuring that the types of\0Aany returned values, and the types expected by callers, match expectation.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Adef add(a, b):\0A    return a + b\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Adef add(a: int, b: int) -> int:\0A    return a + b\0A```\0A\0A## Availability\0A\0ABecause this rule relies on the third-party `typing_extensions` module for some Python versions,\0Aits diagnostic will not be emitted, and no fix will be offered, if `typing_extensions` imports\0Ahave been disabled by the [`lint.typing-extensions`] linter option.\0A\0A## Options\0A\0A- `lint.typing-extensions`\0A", align 1
