@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1163 = private unnamed_addr constant [909 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks that \22special\22 methods, like `__init__`, `__new__`, and `__call__`, have\0Areturn type annotations.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AType annotations are a good way to document the return types of functions. They also\0Ahelp catch bugs, when used alongside a type checker, by ensuring that the types of\0Aany returned values, and the types expected by callers, match expectation.\0A\0ANote that type checkers often allow you to omit the return type annotation for\0A`__init__` methods, as long as at least one argument has a type annotation. To\0Aopt in to this behavior, use the `mypy-init-return` setting in your `pyproject.toml`\0Aor `ruff.toml` file:\0A\0A```toml\0A[tool.ruff.lint.flake8-annotations]\0Amypy-init-return = true\0A```\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aclass Foo:\0A    def __init__(self, x: int):\0A        self.x = x\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aclass Foo:\0A    def __init__(self, x: int) -> None:\0A        self.x = x\0A```\0A", align 1
