@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1181 = private unnamed_addr constant [1034 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks that function arguments are annotated with a more specific type than\0A`Any`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`Any` is a special type indicating an unconstrained type. When an\0Aexpression is annotated with type `Any`, type checkers will allow all\0Aoperations on it.\0A\0AIt's better to be explicit about the type of an expression, and to use\0A`Any` as an \22escape hatch\22 only when it is really needed.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Adef foo(x: Any): ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Adef foo(x: int): ...\0A```\0A\0A## Known problems\0A\0AType aliases are unsupported and can lead to false positives.\0AFor example, the following will trigger this rule inadvertently:\0A\0A```python\0Afrom typing import Any\0A\0AMyAny = Any\0A\0A\0Adef foo(x: MyAny): ...\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Typing spec: `Any`](https://typing.python.org/en/latest/spec/special-types.html#any)\0A- [Python documentation: `typing.Any`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/typing.html#typing.Any)\0A- [Mypy documentation: The Any type](https://mypy.readthedocs.io/en/stable/kinds_of_types.html#the-any-type)\0A", align 1
