@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1347 = private unnamed_addr constant [674 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for explicit casts to `list` on for-loop iterables.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AUsing a `list()` call to eagerly iterate over an already-iterable type\0A(like a tuple, list, or set) is inefficient, as it forces Python to create\0Aa new list unnecessarily.\0A\0ARemoving the `list()` call will not change the behavior of the code, but\0Amay improve performance.\0A\0ANote that, as with all `perflint` rules, this is only intended as a\0Amicro-optimization, and will have a negligible impact on performance in\0Amost cases.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aitems = (1, 2, 3)\0Afor i in list(items):\0A    print(i)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aitems = (1, 2, 3)\0Afor i in items:\0A    print(i)\0A```\0A", align 1
