@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1385 = private unnamed_addr constant [1291 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for public `__init__` method definitions that are missing\0Adocstrings.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0APublic `__init__` methods are used to initialize objects. `__init__`\0Amethods should be documented via docstrings to describe the method's\0Abehavior, arguments, side effects, exceptions, and any other information\0Athat may be relevant to the user.\0A\0AIf the codebase adheres to a standard format for `__init__` method docstrings,\0Afollow that format for consistency.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aclass City:\0A    def __init__(self, name: str, population: int) -> None:\0A        self.name: str = name\0A        self.population: int = population\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aclass City:\0A    def __init__(self, name: str, population: int) -> None:\0A        \22\22\22Initialize a city with a name and population.\22\22\22\0A        self.name: str = name\0A        self.population: int = population\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `lint.pydocstyle.ignore-decorators`\0A\0A## References\0A- [PEP 257 \E2\80\93 Docstring Conventions](https://peps.python.org/pep-0257/)\0A- [PEP 287 \E2\80\93 reStructuredText Docstring Format](https://peps.python.org/pep-0287/)\0A- [NumPy Style Guide](https://numpydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/format.html)\0A- [Google Style Python Docstrings](https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#s3.8-comments-and-docstrings)\0A", align 1
