@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1452 = private unnamed_addr constant [1359 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for explicit use of dunder methods, like `__str__` and `__add__`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ADunder names are not meant to be called explicitly and, in most cases, can\0Abe replaced with builtins or operators.\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis fix is always unsafe. When replacing dunder method calls with operators\0Aor builtins, the behavior can change in the following ways:\0A\0A1. Types may implement only a subset of related dunder methods. Calling a\0A   missing dunder method directly returns `NotImplemented`, but using the\0A   equivalent operator raises a `TypeError`.\0A   ```python\0A   class C: pass\0A   c = C()\0A   c.__gt__(1)  # before fix: NotImplemented\0A   c > 1        # after fix: raises TypeError\0A   ```\0A2. Instance-assigned dunder methods are ignored by operators and builtins.\0A   ```python\0A   class C: pass\0A   c = C()\0A   c.__bool__ = lambda: False\0A   c.__bool__() # before fix: False\0A   bool(c)      # after fix: True\0A   ```\0A\0A3. Even with built-in types, behavior can differ.\0A   ```python\0A   (1).__gt__(1.0)  # before fix: NotImplemented\0A   1 > 1.0          # after fix: False\0A   ```\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Athree = (3.0).__str__()\0Atwelve = \221\22.__add__(\222\22)\0A\0A\0Adef is_greater_than_two(x: int) -> bool:\0A    return x.__gt__(2)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Athree = str(3.0)\0Atwelve = \221\22 + \222\22\0A\0A\0Adef is_greater_than_two(x: int) -> bool:\0A    return x > 2\0A```\0A\0A", align 1
